      "batch_size": 1,
      "hardware_label": "RTX 3080",
      "notes": "Muse-Glimmer-30B UD-Q3_K_XL (12.44 GiB), 52 layers. Does NOT fit 10GB: 40 layers on GPU, 12 on CPU (planned offload, not driver paging - shared host mem stays at 124 MiB). Those 12 CPU layers cost dearly: 13.78 tok/s, and PCIe rx peaks at 20.3 GB/s streaming them every token. ngl 36 measured 9.83 tok/s, so each GPU layer is worth ~1 tok/s here; ngl 40 leaves 632 MiB headroom and is the practical ceiling. Medians of 2 reps. If you have 10GB, a smaller fully-resident quant beats this by 5x. Headless 3080 (both displays on the 7900X iGPU; desktop holds ~64 MiB on the card). Workload: single non-repetitive synthetic log-summarisation prompt, 256 output tokens, temp 0, cache_prompt off, medians of 3 reps (2 where noted). Prefill/decode from llama-server timings; TTFT measured client-side on a streaming request. Prompt token counts differ per model because tokenizers differ. llama.cpp b10472 (60eeeb608), Windows 11 / CUDA 12.4, driver 591.86, PCIe 4.0 x16, stock 370 W.",
      "observed_command": "llama-server -m Muse-Glimmer-30B-UD-Q3_K_XL.gguf -c 16384 -ngl 40 -fa on -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0 --parallel 1 --jinja --spec-type none",
